      <h2>4. Promotional Jumps: Horizontal Movement and MACP Rules</h2>
      <p>
        One of the greatest achievements of the Pay Matrix system is the transparency it brings to departmental promotions and <strong>Modified Assured Career Progression (MACP)</strong> upgradations (awarded at 10, 20, and 30 years of continuous service).
      </p>
      
      <h3>The Exact Promotion Arithmetic in the Matrix</h3>
      <p>
        When an employee is promoted from a lower level to a higher level (e.g., from Level 6 to Level 7), their new salary is not determined by arbitrary negotiation. Instead, it follows a standardized two-step process:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Granting Promotional Increment:</strong> Locate the employee’s existing cell in their current Level. Move vertically down by <em>one cell</em> within that same Level (granting a standard 3% promotional increment).</li>
        <li><strong>Fitting into the Higher Level:</strong> Take the resulting basic pay from Step 1 and look across at the newly promoted Level. Locate the cell that is exactly equal to that figure. If no identical cell exists, the pay is fitted into the <strong>immediate next higher cell</strong> in that level.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="formula-banner">
        Promotion Rule: Current Cell + 1 Vertical Increment in Same Level → Fit into Next Higher Value Cell in Promoted Level
      </div>

      <h2>5. Compression Ratios: Balancing Entry Wages Against Apex Executive Scales</h2>
      <p>
        A core responsibility of every Central Pay Commission is recalibrating the <strong>compression ratio</strong>—the arithmetic ratio between the lowest basic salary in Level 1 (MTS / Group D) and the highest salary ceiling in Level 18 (Cabinet Secretary of India).
      </p>
      <p>
        Historical compression ratios across Pay Commissions reveal shifting fiscal priorities:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1st Pay Commission (1946):</strong> Compression ratio of <strong>1:38</strong> (Lowest pay: ₹55; Highest pay: ₹2,000).</li>
        <li><strong>5th Pay Commission (1996):</strong> Compression ratio compressed down to approximately <strong>1:10.7</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>6th Pay Commission (2006):</strong> Ratio relaxed to approximately <strong>1:11.4</strong> (₹7,000 to ₹80,000).</li>
        <li><strong>7th Pay Commission (2016):</strong> Ratio established at <strong>1:13.9</strong> (₹18,000 minimum basic in Level 1 to ₹2,50,000 maximum basic in Level 18).</li>
        <li><strong>8th Pay Commission Projections:</strong> Staff federations strongly advocate for a compression ratio of between <strong>1:12 and 1:13</strong>, seeking to lift the lower floor (Level 1) significantly to counteract food, urban housing, and educational cost inflation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. Annual Increments: Compounding Dynamics (January 1 vs. July 1)</h2>
      <p>
        Under Central Government civil service rules, annual increments are uniform and standardized across two universal qualifying dates:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>January 1st Cycle:</strong> Applicable to employees appointed, promoted, or granted MACP between January 2nd and July 1st of the preceding year.</li>
        <li><strong>July 1st Cycle:</strong> Applicable to employees appointed, promoted, or granted MACP between July 2nd and January 1st of the preceding year.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because each cell in the Pay Matrix compounds the preceding basic by approximately 3%, employees who complete long tenures in a single level receive progressively larger absolute rupee increments with each passing year. For example, in Level 6, early increments add approximately ₹1,100 per month to basic pay, whereas employees in Cell 25 receive increments exceeding ₹2,500 per month.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Stagnation Relief: Terminal Cell Rules and Biennial Increments</h2>
      <p>
        A critical structural challenge in earlier pay commissions was <strong>salary stagnation</strong>: when an employee served in a post for decades without functional promotion, they eventually reached the maximum ceiling of their Pay Band.
      </p>
      <p>
        The 7th CPC addressed this by providing between <strong>34 and 40 sequential cells</strong> for all regular operational cadres (Levels 1 to 12). If an employee reaches the final cell of their matrix level without further promotion, service rules provide for <em>stagnation increments</em>: granting additional biennial (once every two years) increments up to a statutory ceiling, ensuring dedicated personnel never experience a permanent freeze in base remuneration. The 8th CPC is expected to expand cell capacities in middle-management cadres (Levels 13 and 14) to prevent stagnation in technical leadership roles.
      </p>

